"7 The phrase “he who communes with the excommunicate, let him be excommunicate” concisely renders the canonical principle expressly formulated in Canon 2 of the Synod of Antioch (341). The authentic text reads as follows: “Let it not be permitted to commune with the excommunicate (in any kind of communion)-, nor to pray together at home with those who do not pray together with the Church… And if any of the bishops, or presbyters, or deacons, or anyone of the canon be found communing with the excommunicate, let him also be excommunicate, as one who confuses the canon of the Church.” And Saint Theodore the Studite, Epistle 39, “To Theophilos the abbot”, and in Patrologia Graeca, PG 99, 1049A–B. The text of the epistle reads as follows: “For Chrysostom declared with a great and loud voice that enemies of God are not only the heretics, but also those who commune with such persons.”",
"that I have erred concerning faith in God, in whatever way! (that I have erred around the faith — that toward God — in any way whatsoever). ...The Holy Spirit through the Apostle anathematizes even angels who legislate anything contrary to the preaching (insofar as they legislate things opposed to the preaching of the Apostles).” With threats and flatteries the interrogators under Theodosius of Caesarea tried to bend the mind of Saint Maximus, so that he would submit to the Monothelite heresy, but he remained firm in the Confession of the Faith, “unswerving, wholly steadfast and immovable in mind.” Cunningly Theodosius tried to persuade the Great Confessor that they agreed with him, that they were not changing the dogma, but simply managing matters by economy with the “Typos,” so that the “church” and the country might be at peace: “But what has been done by economy must not be taken as a dogma proper, as indeed the typos now proposed by us was made by economy, but not dogmatically.” On the answer of the great Maximus was founded also the stance of the other great saints thereafter, of Saint Gregory Palamas and of Saint Mark of Ephesus. In matters of faith there is no room for condescension and economy or compromise. Heresy is identified with falsehood and those who try to beautify it with economies are false teachers and deceivers. We ought not to obey them, but on the contrary to turn away from them and keep away from them, lest it appear that by our association we partake in their heresy and in their wickedness: “Such is the way of false teachers and deceivers, whom one must not even obey, but turn away from, as far as possible, and keep distant, lest we also seem to enjoy some evil from their company.”",
